A LinkedHashSet is suitable because:
- It does not allow duplicate elements.
- It preserves insertion order.

plain java
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 5};

Set<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();

for (int num : arr) {
    set.add(num);
}

int[] result = new int[set.size()];

int index = 0;

for (int num : set) {
    result[index++] = num;
}

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));



java 8
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 5};

int[] result = Arrays.stream(arr)
        .distinct()
        .toArray();

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));

D. Complexity
Solution	Time	Extra space
LinkedHashSet	O(n) average	O(n)
Java 8 distinct	O(n) average	O(n)

E. Choice of solution
Use LinkedHashSet for explicit collection-based logic.

Use distinct() when writing Java 8 Stream code.

